<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-255: the arranger's horizontal scroll bar follows the canvas' pan, so a
  RESTORED pan does not leave the thumb at the far left.

  THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE FIXTURE. `loadViewStateFromProject()` runs inside
  SMVActualView's constructor, so the only way to reach it is to have the
  project's properties already in place when the arranger is BUILT. That is
  what every startup does: main()'s interactive path is `openMostRecent()`,
  whose own comment says it "creates the project and with it the central
  widget (the mixer view)". So nothing here may touch the arranger before the
  load: `load-project` is the first action, and the `assert-lane-view` right
  after it is what constructs the view.

  Measured before the fix, with a probe at the end of the constructor:

      hbar.value=0  hbar.max=321759  canvas.leftOffset=123456

  The bar's maximum already had room for 123456, so this was never a clamp:
  the value was simply never set. `loadViewStateFromProject()` assigns
  `upperLeftOffset_` directly rather than through `setLeftOffset()`, so no
  `leftOffsetChanged` is emitted, and it runs before SStdMixerView has wired
  that signal to `avLeftOffsetChanged()` at all.

  WHY track_list_view_roundtrip.qxa CANNOT SEE THIS, although it reads the
  same two numbers back after a save/load: `load-project` loads INTO the live
  project and does not rebuild the arranger (measured: the constructor runs
  exactly once in that case, before the save), so its post-load assertions
  are satisfied by the same view `set-lane-view` had already moved. The
  restore path never runs there. A committed fixture loaded by a case that
  has built no arranger yet is the only shape that reaches it.

  `hScroll` is the scroll bar's own `value()`, in the same project frames as
  `scrollX`; QBX-255 adds it to `arrangerDescribeView()` because the describe
  string used to report the canvas alone, which is why no assertion could see
  the thumb at all.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="arranger_scrollbar_follows_restored_pan">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- FIRST, and nothing before it: the fixture's properties carry
         timelineZoomSecondWidth=45.5 and timelineScrollX=123456, and no
         arranger exists yet to read them. -->
    <load-project path="../arranger_view_state_restore.qxp"/>

    <!-- This is what builds the arranger. The canvas restores the pan
         (scrollX) and the thumb must be in the same place (hScroll). Both
         EXACT: these are project frames the fixture states, not a wheel
         landing position, so no user setting can move them. -->
    <assert-lane-view secondWidth="45.5" scrollX="123456" hScroll="123456"/>

    <!-- The bar is not merely parked at its own maximum by luck: pan back to
         the start through the ordinary setter and the thumb follows there
         too, which is the path that always worked and must keep working. -->
    <set-lane-view scrollX="0"/>
    <assert-lane-view scrollX="0" hScroll="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <!-- The fixture really loaded: two top-level tracks. -->
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
